<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z" agent="Trae"><diagram id="pipeline" name="Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="2600" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2200" pageHeight="3200" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" /><mxCell id="header" value="SciAtlas Idea Generation Pipeline&#10;Run dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_question/04-&#10;未来能源存储/full&#10;Topic: Research question: What is the future for energy&#10;storage?&#10;Context: If we are to become more reliant on renewable&#10;energy such as wind and solar, we need to be able to store&#10;it efficiently and inexpensively. Storage is especially&#10;important, given the somewhat unpredictable nature of&#10;these energy sources. One of the most promising&#10;opportunities for energy storage is batteries. Invented in&#10;1800, batteries have evolved into the convenient, handheld&#10;models to which we are now accustomed. Today, scientists&#10;with backgrounds in chemistry, materials science,&#10;electrical and mechanical engineering, and other fields&#10;have contributed to the advent of what some call the “Ion&#10;Age”—a new era of batteries that use lithium ions for&#10;their electrochemistry. The 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry&#10;was awarded in recognition of this innovation. There are&#10;many benefits to lithium-ion batteries. They are energy&#10;efficient, have a high energy density, provide an&#10;abundance of current for high-power applications, are&#10;generally low cost, and are easily maintainable. They also&#10;have a long life cycle, a key component to employing them&#10;in large-scale energy storage. But the advantages of&#10;batteries extend beyond the electrical grid: “Because&#10;improving battery technology is essential to the&#10;widespread use of plug-in electric vehicles, storage is&#10;also key to reducing our dependency on petroleum for&#10;transportation,” as noted by the U.S. Department of&#10;Energy. Among the impediments to widespread application of&#10;large-scale batteries for grid-level energy storage is&#10;access to raw materials. Lithium-ion batteries require&#10;cobalt, considered a “conflict mineral” because of the&#10;negative environmental and social ramifications associated&#10;with mining it, especially in the Democratic Republic of&#10;Congo, which supplies 60% of the world’s cobalt.&#10;Noncobalt, nonmetal cathodes, such as sulfur- based&#10;electrodes, offer great promise for the next generation of&#10;batteries. Lithium is mainly procured from Australia,&#10;Argentina, and Chile, where concerns over unethical labor&#10;practices and water consumption plague the industry.&#10;Advanced technologies for lithium extraction and battery&#10;recycling—or substituting lithium with, for example,&#10;sodium—are essential to alleviate these resource&#10;challenges. Worldwide, there are many battery technology&#10;advancement efforts coalescing across industry,&#10;government, and academia. In China, there are 93&#10;“gigafactories” that manufacture lithium-ion battery&#10;cells, according to Benchmark Mineral Intelligence. But&#10;lithium-ion batteries are just one possible solution for&#10;energy storage. Technologists are also experimenting with&#10;flow batteries (liquid batteries), pumped hydro, stacked&#10;blocks (which rely on gravity), and compressed air and&#10;liquid air, among others.&#10;Status: ok&#10;Failed step: N/A" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="40" width="442" height="1176" /></mxCell><mxCell id="params" value="Input Parameters&#10;topic: Research question: What is the future for energy&#10;storage?&#10;Context: If we are to become more reliant on renewable&#10;energy such as wind and solar, we need to be able to store&#10;it efficiently and inexpensively. Storage is especially&#10;important, given the somewhat unpredictable nature of&#10;these energy sources. One of the most promising&#10;opportunities for energy storage is batteries. Invented in&#10;1800, batteries have evolved into the convenient, handheld&#10;models to which we are now accustomed. Today, scientists&#10;with backgrounds in chemistry, materials science,&#10;electrical and mechanical engineering, and other fields&#10;have contributed to the advent of what some call the “Ion&#10;Age”—a new era of batteries that use lithium ions for&#10;their electrochemistry. The 2019 Nobel Prize in Chemistry&#10;was awarded in recognition of this innovation. There are&#10;many benefits to lithium-ion batteries. They are energy&#10;efficient, have a high energy density, provide an&#10;abundance of current for high-power applications, are&#10;generally low cost, and are easily maintainable. They also&#10;have a long life cycle, a key component to employing them&#10;in large-scale energy storage. But the advantages of&#10;batteries extend beyond the electrical grid: “Because&#10;improving battery technology is essential to the&#10;widespread use of plug-in electric vehicles, storage is&#10;also key to reducing our dependency on petroleum for&#10;transportation,” as noted by the U.S. Department of&#10;Energy. Among the impediments to widespread application of&#10;large-scale batteries for grid-level energy storage is&#10;access to raw materials. Lithium-ion batteries require&#10;cobalt, considered a “conflict mineral” because of the&#10;negative environmental and social ramifications associated&#10;with mining it, especially in the Democratic Republic of&#10;Congo, which supplies 60% of the world’s cobalt.&#10;Noncobalt, nonmetal cathodes, such as sulfur- based&#10;electrodes, offer great promise for the next generation of&#10;batteries. Lithium is mainly procured from Australia,&#10;Argentina, and Chile, where concerns over unethical labor&#10;practices and water consumption plague the industry.&#10;Advanced technologies for lithium extraction and battery&#10;recycling—or substituting lithium with, for example,&#10;sodium—are essential to alleviate these resource&#10;challenges. Worldwide, there are many battery technology&#10;advancement efforts coalescing across industry,&#10;government, and academia. In China, there are 93&#10;“gigafactories” that manufacture lithium-ion battery&#10;cells, according to Benchmark Mineral Intelligence. But&#10;lithium-ion batteries are just one possible solution for&#10;energy storage. Technologists are also experimenting with&#10;flow batteries (liquid batteries), pumped hydro, stacked&#10;blocks (which rely on gravity), and compressed air and&#10;liquid air, among others.&#10;domain: Chemistry&#10;resume_from_run_dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_q&#10;uestion/04-未来能源存储/full&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;&#10;Effective Pipeline Config&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;anchor_top_k: 5&#10;keyword_min_papers: 2&#10;k_step1: 4&#10;seed_num_queries: 8&#10;seed_per_query: 3&#10;seed_request_multiplier: 6&#10;seed_request_floor: 12&#10;seed_recent_years: 7&#10;seed_min_citations: 1000&#10;graph_budget_ratio: 0.35&#10;graph_budget_min: 12&#10;graph_budget_max: 30&#10;graph_max_predecessors_per_paper: 3&#10;graph_min_forward_papers: 3&#10;graph_candidate_fetch_limit: 40&#10;num_candidate_step2: 3&#10;num_expansion_step2: 1&#10;innoeval_root: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/SciAtlas/referen&#10;ces/search/src/innoeval_search&#10;grobid_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8070&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_same_field: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;max_novelty_feedback_rounds: 2&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;runs_dir: runs&#10;&#10;Effective SciAtlas Config&#10;base_url: http://sciatlas.openkg.cn&#10;api_key: ***REDACTED***&#10;timeout: 900&#10;max_retries: 4&#10;retry_backoff: 5.0&#10;use_cache: True&#10;cache_dir: runs/cache&#10;official_cli_root: .&#10;use_local_kg: False&#10;local_search_root: references/search" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="40" width="442" height="1950" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step1" value="Step 1 Anchor Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;seed_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="340" width="220" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step2" value="Step 2 Research Graph Construction&#10;status: completed&#10;graph_paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="500" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step1_step_step2" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="step_step2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step2_graph_title" value="Step 2 Annotated Research Graph&#10;paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0&#10;phase_count: 1&#10;directions: anchor=4, backward=0,&#10;forward=0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=13;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="500" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/4&#10;Leveraging Neural Networks and&#10;Genetic Algorithms to Refine&#10;Electrode Properties in Redox Flow&#10;Batteries&#10;year: 2021&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4242739072&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="196" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_1" value="Seed Paper 2/4&#10;Electrochemical energy storage in&#10;formate ions&#10;year: 2021&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4234705137&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="640" y="672" width="260" height="148" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_1" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;Sorting Lithium-Ion Battery&#10;Electrode Materials Using&#10;Dielectrophoresis&#10;year: 2023&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4384282629&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;Novel Sulfide‐Chloride Solid‐State&#10;Electrolytes with Tunable Anion&#10;Ratio for Highly Stable&#10;Solid‐State Sodium‐Ion Batteries&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4410507001&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="196" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 8173&#10;trend_chars: 8359" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="968" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: What is the future for&#10;energy storage? Context: If we are to become more reliant on&#10;renewable energy such as wind and solar, we need to be able to&#10;store it efficiently and inexpensively. Storage is especially&#10;important, given the somewhat unpredictable nature of these&#10;energy sources. One of the most promising opportunities for&#10;energy storage is batteries. Invented in 1800, batteries have&#10;evolved into the convenient, handheld models to which we are&#10;now accustomed. Today, scientists with backgrounds in&#10;chemistry, materials science, electrical and mechanical&#10;engineering, and other fields have contributed to the advent&#10;of what some call the “Ion Age”—a new era of batteries that&#10;use lithium ions for their electrochemistry. The 2019 Nobel&#10;Prize in Chemistry was awarded in recognition of this&#10;innovation. There are many benefits to lithium-ion batteries.&#10;They are energy efficient, have a high energy density, provide&#10;an abundance of current for high-power applications, are&#10;generally low cost, and are easily maintainable. They also&#10;have a long life cycle, a key component to employing them in&#10;large-scale energy storage. But the advantages of batteries&#10;extend beyond the electrical grid: “Because improving battery&#10;technology is essential to the widespread use of plug-in&#10;electric vehicles, storage is also key to reducing our&#10;dependency on petroleum for transportation,” as noted by the&#10;U.S. Department of Energy. Among the impediments to widespread&#10;application of large-scale batteries for grid-level energy&#10;storage is access to raw materials. Lithium-ion batteries&#10;require cobalt, considered a “conflict mineral” because of the&#10;negative environmental and social ramifications associated&#10;with mining it, especially in the Democratic Republic of&#10;Congo, which supplies 60% of the world’s cobalt. Noncobalt,&#10;nonmetal cathodes, such as sulfur- based electrodes, offer&#10;great promise for the next generation of batteries. Lithium is&#10;mainly procured from Australia, Argentina, and Chile, where&#10;concerns over unethical labor practices and water consumption&#10;plague the industry. Advanced technologies for lithium&#10;extraction and battery recycling—or substituting lithium with,&#10;for example, sodium—are essential to alleviate these resource&#10;challenges. Worldwide, there are many battery technology&#10;advancement efforts coalescing across industry, government,&#10;and academia. In China, there are 93 “gigafactories” that&#10;manufacture lithium-ion battery cells, according to Benchmark&#10;Mineral Intelligence. But lithium-ion batteries are just one&#10;possible solution for energy storage. Technologists are also&#10;experimenting with flow batteries (liquid batteries), pumped&#10;hydro, stacked blocks (which rely on gravity), and compressed&#10;air and liquid air, among others. seed_paper: Leveraging&#10;Neural Networks and Genetic Algorithms to Refine Electrode&#10;Properties in Redox Flow Batteries # CITATION SUBGRAPH (3&#10;refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1] Electrochemical&#10;energy storage in formate ions (2021, venue=N/A) authors: N/A&#10;[EDGE] layer_depth = 1 cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 0&#10;section_weight = 0.0 delta_year = unknown is_influential =&#10;False low_confidence = True...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: Paper 0 to Paper 1: Using the numbering Paper 0 = seed&#10;paper, Paper 1 = ref [1], Paper 2 = ref [2], Paper 3 = ref&#10;[3]. The seed paper represents a computational-design stage in&#10;energy-storage research: neural networks and genetic&#10;algorithms are used to refine electrode properties in redox&#10;flow batteries. Its focus is performance improvement through&#10;optimization of electrode structure and properties within an&#10;existing flow-battery platform. Paper 1, “Electrochemical&#10;energy storage in formate ions,” shifts the emphasis from&#10;optimizing a device component to rethinking the storage&#10;chemistry itself. The progression is from electrode-level&#10;engineering toward molecular/liquid energy carriers, where&#10;energy is stored through reversible electrochemical&#10;transformations of formate ions. This matters because it&#10;broadens the future-storage landscape beyond conventional&#10;battery electrodes and points toward potentially low-cost,&#10;chemically flexible storage media for renewable electricity.&#10;Paper 1 to Paper 2: Paper 2, “Sorting Lithium-Ion Battery&#10;Electrode Materials Using Dielectrophoresis,” moves the&#10;discussion from alternative storage chemistries to the&#10;sustainability and circularity of the dominant lithium-ion&#10;technology. The transition is from “what new chemistry can&#10;store renewable energy?” to “how can we recover and reuse the&#10;materials already embedded in large-scale lithium-ion&#10;deployment?” The concrete advance is the use of&#10;dielectrophoresis to sort electrode materials, which addresses&#10;recycling, resource recovery, and reduced dependence on virgin&#10;raw materials. This matters because even if next-generation&#10;chemistries emerge, lithium-ion batteries remain central to&#10;near-term grid and vehicle storage, and their environmental&#10;and supply-chain burdens must be reduced. Paper 2 to Paper 3:&#10;Paper 3, “Novel Sulfide-Chloride Solid-State Electrolytes with&#10;Tunable Anion Ratio for Highly Stable Solid-State Sodium-Ion&#10;Batteries,” advances the lineage from recycling incumbent&#10;lithium-ion materials to replacing lithium with sodium and&#10;moving from conventional liquid-electrolyte designs toward&#10;solid-state architectures. The concrete advance is the&#10;development of tunable sulfide-chloride solid-state&#10;electrolytes aimed at improving ionic conductivity,&#10;electrochemical stability, and cell durability. This matters&#10;because sodium is more abundant than lithium, and solid-&#10;state..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="968" width="560" height="2022" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3090" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Electrochemical energy storage for renewable&#10;electricity, including redox flow batteries, lithium-ion&#10;batteries, formate-based electrochemical storage, lithium-ion&#10;material recycling, and solid-state sodium-ion batteries.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Existing storage approaches improve isolated&#10;elements—flow-battery electrode properties, alternative&#10;formate carriers, lithium-ion material sorting, or solid-state&#10;sodium electrolytes—but do not provide a coupled scientific&#10;method for simultaneously stabilizing ion and electron&#10;transport at interfaces while meeting energy efficiency,&#10;safety, resource abundance, recyclability, and scalable&#10;lifetime. The missing capability is co-design of materials,&#10;interfaces, degradation mechanisms, and lifecycle constraints.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- intermittent renewable electricity from wind and solar&#10;- redox flow batteries&#10;- flow-battery electrode properties&#10;- neural networks and genetic algorithms for electrode&#10;optimization&#10;- formate ions as reversible electrochemical energy carriers&#10;- lithium-ion batteries&#10;- lithium-ion electrode materials&#10;- spent lithium-ion battery materials&#10;- dielectrophoresis-based electrode-material sorting&#10;- solid-state sodium-ion batteries&#10;- sulfide-chloride solid-state electrolytes&#10;- tunable anion ratio in solid electrolytes&#10;- sodium ions as lithium substitutes&#10;- lithium and cobalt resource constraints&#10;- electrode-electrolyte interfaces&#10;- ionic conductivity&#10;- electrochemical stability&#10;- cycle life&#10;- energy density&#10;- safety&#10;- cost&#10;- recyclability&#10;- scalable manufacturing&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- intermittent renewable electricity creates demand for&#10;efficient and inexpensive storage&#10;- redox flow batteries store energy through reversible&#10;electrochemical reactions at electrodes and in liquid redox&#10;media&#10;- neural networks and genetic algorithms can refine flow-&#10;battery electrode properties to improve performance&#10;- formate ions can act as alternative electrochemical energy&#10;carriers beyond conventional battery electrodes&#10;- lithium-ion batteries remain central to near-term grid and&#10;vehicle storage but depend on constrained raw materials&#10;- dielectrophoresis can sort spent lithium-ion electrode&#10;materials to support recycling and circular material supply&#10;- recycling lithium-ion materials can reduce demand for virgin&#10;lithium and cobalt but does not fully remove dependence on&#10;primary resources&#10;- solid-state sodium-ion batteries substitute sodium for&#10;lithium to reduce resource constraints&#10;- sulfide-chloride solid-state electrolytes with tunable anion&#10;ratios aim to improve ionic conductivity and electrochemical&#10;stability&#10;- electrode-electrolyte interfaces govern charge transport,&#10;side reactions, resistance growth, and long-term durability&#10;- computational optimization becomes more scientifically&#10;useful when coupled with realistic ion transport, degradation,&#10;and recycling models&#10;- future storage systems must connect electrochemical&#10;performance with material abundance, safety, recyclability,&#10;and manufacturability&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- interfacial charge-transfer resistance and degradation&#10;across battery and flow-cell architectures&#10;- insufficient long-term stability of alternative storage&#10;chemistries such as formate-based systems&#10;- difficulty restoring electrochemical function during direct&#10;recycling of spent electrode materials&#10;- instability of solid-state sodium battery interfaces,&#10;including sodium-metal compatibility, dendrite suppression,&#10;interdiffusion, and chemomechanical stress&#10;- limited scalability of computational optimization,&#10;dielectrophoretic sorting, and tunable solid electrolytes to&#10;gigawatt-hour deployment&#10;- lack of physics-informed data-driven tools that predict&#10;decades of operation, failure, and recyclability&#10;- persistent dependence on lithium, cobalt, and other&#10;constrained raw materials&#10;- no single demonstrated approach simultaneously resolves&#10;energy density, cost, safety, cycle life, scalability, and&#10;resource sustainability&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- high energy density versus use of abundant, low-cost,&#10;noncritical materials&#10;- improved safety through solid-state architectures versus&#10;manufacturability and interfacial performance&#10;- recycling incumbent lithium-ion systems versus developing&#10;post-lithium chemistries from the outset&#10;- low-cost alternative chemistries versus long cycle life and&#10;high round-trip efficiency&#10;- computational acceleration of material design versus&#10;experimentally grounded degradation and recycling realism&#10;- material sorting and recovery versus functional restoration&#10;without energy-intensive reprocessing&#10;- device-level performance optimization versus lifecycle&#10;sustainability and circularity&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- high energy density and sufficient power capability&#10;- high round-trip energy efficiency&#10;- long cycle life and calendar life&#10;- low cost and scalable manufacturing&#10;- safe operation under grid and vehicle conditions&#10;- use of abundant, non-cobalt, non-lithium, or circularly&#10;recovered materials&#10;- stable ion and electron transport with minimal interfacial&#10;degradation&#10;- recyclable or directly regenerable electrode and electrolyte&#10;materials&#10;- predictable long-term performance under realistic operating&#10;conditions&#10;- compatibility with large-scale renewable energy storage and&#10;electric transportation&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- multi-objective physics-informed co-design of electrodes,&#10;electrolytes, interfaces, and lifecycle constraints&#10;- interfacial passivation or buffer-layer mechanisms that&#10;maintain transport while suppressing side reactions and&#10;dendrites&#10;- compositionally tunable solid electrolytes for stable&#10;sodium-ion conduction&#10;- reversible abundant-element redox carriers using formate,&#10;sulfur, or other noncritical chemistries&#10;- field-assisted or electrochemical separation mechanisms for&#10;direct recovery and restoration of electrode materials&#10;- degradation-aware machine learning and evolutionary&#10;optimization coupled to transport, failure, and recycling&#10;models&#10;- chemomechanically compliant solid-state architectures that&#10;tolerate stress and interfacial volume change&#10;- closed-loop material recovery mechanisms that preserve or&#10;upgrade electrochemical function&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- solid-state ionics, ceramic grain-boundary engineering, and&#10;defect chemistry&#10;- corrosion passivation, self-healing coatings, and artificial&#10;solid-electrolyte interphase design&#10;- fuel-cell and electrolyzer membrane-electrode interface&#10;stabilization methods&#10;- organic redox chemistry and electrocatalysis for formate,&#10;sulfur, or liquid energy carriers&#10;- porous electrode transport and flow-reactor engineering for&#10;redox flow batteries&#10;- mineral processing, hydrometallurgy, and electrostatic or&#10;dielectrophoretic separation for battery recycling&#10;- chemomechanics and fracture mechanics for brittle solid&#10;electrolytes&#10;- physics-informed digital twins and accelerated aging&#10;protocols for long-life storage systems&#10;- sodium-ion, sulfur-based, and other resource-abundant&#10;electrochemical storage chemistries" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3090" width="560" height="3138" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: completed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6328" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed to remain grounded&#10;in electrochemical storage feasibility, including batteries,&#10;flow systems, solid electrolytes, and recycling. Cross-domain&#10;retrieval is justified because the core bottlenecks involve&#10;interfacial stability, chemomechanical failure, direct&#10;material restoration, and lifecycle-aware optimization, where&#10;mechanisms from corrosion passivation, membrane-electrode&#10;systems, mineral processing, fracture mechanics, or digital&#10;twins may transfer." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6328" width="560" height="312" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6740" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Understanding Battery Interfaces by Combined&#10;Characterization and Simulation Approaches: Challenges and&#10;Perspectives&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Abstract Driven by the continuous search for&#10;improving performances, understanding the phenomena at the&#10;electrode/electrolyte interfaces has become an overriding&#10;factor for the success of sustainable and efficient battery&#10;technologies for mobile and stationary applications. To...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Post-Lithium Battery Technologies Driving the Future of&#10;Eco-Conscious Electric Vehicles&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: As electric vehicles (EVs) transition from niche&#10;adoption to mass-market reality, the underlying lithium-ion&#10;battery (LIB) architecture has begun to show limitations in&#10;cost, safety, and sustainability. This paper presents a&#10;comprehensive exploration of emerging post-lithium b...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: chemical_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Multiscale Modeling of Electrochemical Reactions and&#10;Processes&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Multiscale Modeling of Electrochemical Reactions and&#10;Processes is a practical guide to multiscale computational&#10;methodologies. It offers a holistic understanding of the&#10;impact of reaction conditions on the overall performance of&#10;electrolyzers, fuel cells, and energy storage de...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: chemical_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Multiscale process systems engineering—analysis and&#10;design of chemical and energy systems from molecular design up&#10;to process optimization&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 5&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Recent Advances in Data-Driven Methods for Degradation&#10;Modeling Across Applications&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Understanding degradation is crucial for ensuring&#10;the longevity and performance of materials, systems, and&#10;organisms. To illustrate the similarities across applications,&#10;this article provides a review of data-based methods in&#10;materials science, engineering, and medicine. The m...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 6&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Iterative Durability Design of Products via&#10;Degradation-Informed Bayesian Optimization&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Green manufacturing has become a pressing issue in&#10;recent years, driven by the acknowledgment of its long-term&#10;social and economic benefits, increasing demand for&#10;environmentally friendly products, and expanding regulations.&#10;One of the key approaches to attaining sustainabilit..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fde9d9;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6740" width="620" height="1392" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step6_candidates_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step6" target="step6_candidates_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step7" value="Step 7 Analogy Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;inspiration_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="8232" width="220" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step6_step_step7" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step6" target="step_step7"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step7_analogy_panel" value="Step 7 Analogy Results&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Understanding Battery Interfaces by Combined&#10;Characterization and Simulation Approaches: Challenges and&#10;Perspectives&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Coupled characterization and simulation&#10;workflow for electrode/electrolyte interfaces', 'Multi-length-&#10;and multi-timescale modeling of charge transfer, ion&#10;migration/diffusion, interphase formation, and interfacial&#10;stability', 'High-throughput electrochemical measurements&#10;combined with spectroscopic diagnostics', 'Lifetime-oriented&#10;interface stability analysis and degradation tracking',&#10;'Standardized, community-coordinated protocols for&#10;battery/interface research']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate’s unified&#10;characterization-simulation workflow into a co-design&#10;framework for storage systems by coupling operando interface&#10;diagnostics, multiscale ion/electron transport models,&#10;degradation mechanisms, and lifecycle constraints. This would&#10;allow simultane...&#10;justification: The candidate paper provides a transferable&#10;organizing principle: integrated, multiscale interface science&#10;combining experiment, simulation, standards, and lifetime&#10;stability analysis. This directly maps onto the original&#10;problem’s need for coupled co-design of materials, inte...&#10;abstract: Abstract Driven by the continuous search for&#10;improving performances, understanding the phenomena at the&#10;electrode/electrolyte interfaces has become an overriding&#10;factor for the success of sustainable and efficient battery&#10;technologies for mobile and stationary applications. To...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Post-Lithium Battery Technologies Driving the Future of&#10;Eco-Conscious Electric Vehicles&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['multi-criteria evaluation of battery&#10;technologies across cost, safety, sustainability, technical&#10;maturity, and infrastructure readiness', 'trade-off mapping&#10;among alternative chemistries based on resource abundance,&#10;environmental impact, and commercial viability', 'strategic&#10;pathway framing for large-scale deployment under lifecycle and&#10;resilience constraints']&#10;combination_plan: Use the candidate’s multi-dimensional&#10;sustainability, safety, resource, and deployment-readiness&#10;framework as an outer co-design loop that constrains and ranks&#10;interface/material strategies according to ion/electron&#10;transport stability, energy efficiency, recyclability, and&#10;sc...&#10;justification: Although the candidate is a survey rather than&#10;a direct interface-engineering method, it provides a&#10;transferable organizing structure for balancing performance,&#10;safety, resource, environmental, and scalability constraints,&#10;which maps directly onto the problem’s need for couple...&#10;abstract: As electric vehicles (EVs) transition from niche&#10;adoption to mass-market reality, the underlying lithium-ion&#10;battery (LIB) architecture has begun to show limitations in&#10;cost, safety, and sustainability. This paper presents a&#10;comprehensive exploration of emerging post-lithium b...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: chemical_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Multiscale Modeling of Electrochemical Reactions and&#10;Processes&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Multiscale modeling hierarchy linking&#10;atomistic/material properties, interface reactions, mesoscale&#10;transport, and device-level performance', 'Coupled treatment&#10;of electrochemical reaction conditions, ion/electron&#10;transport, and efficiency losses', 'Use of computational&#10;models to propagate degradation mechanisms across scales',&#10;'Holistic performance evaluation under operational and&#10;lifecycle constraints']&#10;combination_plan: Adopt the candidate’s multiscale&#10;electrochemical modeling framework as a co-design scaffold,&#10;connecting material selection and interface stability models&#10;to cell-level transport, efficiency, safety, recyclability,&#10;and lifetime constraints. This would allow simultaneous&#10;optimiz...&#10;justification: The candidate provides a transferable&#10;abstraction: a coupled multiscale modeling structure that&#10;relates local electrochemical reactions and interface&#10;processes to system-level performance. This maps directly onto&#10;the problem’s need for co-design of materials, interfaces,&#10;degra...&#10;abstract: Multiscale Modeling of Electrochemical Reactions and&#10;Processes is a practical guide to multiscale computational&#10;methodologies. It offers a holistic understanding of the&#10;impact of reaction conditions on the overall performance of&#10;electrolyzers, fuel cells, and energy storage de...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: chemical_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Multiscale process systems engineering—analysis and&#10;design of chemical and energy systems from molecular design up&#10;to process optimization&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Multiscale process systems engineering&#10;framework linking molecular/material design to process-level&#10;performance', 'Hierarchical co-optimization across length and&#10;time scales: chemistry, interfaces, device operation, and&#10;lifecycle', 'Trade-off analysis among efficiency, safety,&#10;resource constraints, recyclability, and scalability',&#10;'Integration of degradation and process reliability&#10;considerations into early-stage material/interface design']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt multiscale process systems engineering&#10;as a co-design architecture for storage materials, coupling&#10;atomistic/interface transport models with cell-level&#10;efficiency, degradation, safety, recycling, and process&#10;scalability objectives in a unified optimization loop.&#10;justification: The candidate paper’s core&#10;abstraction—systematic design from molecular scale to process&#10;optimization—directly maps onto the problem’s missing coupled&#10;method for simultaneously engineering ion/electron transport,&#10;interfaces, degradation, and lifecycle constraints.&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 5&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Recent Advances in Data-Driven Methods for Degradation&#10;Modeling Across Applications&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Cross-domain degradation-state&#10;representation', 'Data-driven remaining-useful-life and&#10;health-indicator modeling', 'Multi-scale degradation modeling&#10;linking material-level changes to system-level lifetime',&#10;'Physics-informed or hybrid data-driven degradation&#10;surrogates', 'Uncertainty quantification for lifetime and&#10;safety predictions', 'Lifecycle-aware prognostic feedback for&#10;design optimization']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate paper's degradation-&#10;modeling framework to energy-storage co-design by defining&#10;coupled ion/electron transport health indicators at&#10;interfaces, then using data-driven or physics-informed&#10;degradation models to predict how material choices, interface&#10;chemistry...&#10;justification: Although the candidate is a cross-domain review&#10;rather than a storage-specific method, it provides a&#10;transferable abstraction: modeling degradation as a coupled,&#10;multi-scale, lifecycle-governed process. This maps directly&#10;onto the original problem's need to co-design materials...&#10;abstract: Understanding degradation is crucial for ensuring&#10;the longevity and performance of materials, systems, and&#10;organisms. To illustrate the similarities across applications,&#10;this article provides a review of data-based methods in&#10;materials science, engineering, and medicine. The m...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 6&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Iterative Durability Design of Products via&#10;Degradation-Informed Bayesian Optimization&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Degradation-informed Bayesian&#10;optimization as a closed-loop design framework', 'Iterative&#10;durability/lifetime-oriented design under uncertainty',&#10;'Surrogate modeling of degradation trajectories to guide&#10;experiments or simulations', 'Multi-objective trade-off&#10;handling among performance, durability, sustainability, and&#10;cost constraints']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt degradation-informed Bayesian&#10;optimization into a co-design loop for storage materials and&#10;interfaces, where candidate chemistries, interface structures,&#10;and processing parameters are iteratively selected to optimize&#10;coupled ion/electron transport stability, efficiency,...&#10;justification: The candidate provides a transferable&#10;abstraction: using degradation-aware probabilistic&#10;optimization to iteratively design durable products under&#10;competing lifecycle constraints. This maps directly onto the&#10;original problem's need for coupled materials-interface-&#10;degradation-l...&#10;abstract: Green manufacturing has become a pressing issue in&#10;recent years, driven by the acknowledgment of its long-term&#10;social and economic benefits, increasing demand for&#10;environmentally friendly products, and expanding regulations.&#10;One of the key approaches to attaining sustainabilit..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#f9cb9c;strokeColor=#b45f06;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="8232" width="620" height="3588" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step7_analogy_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step7" target="step7_analogy_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step8" value="Step 8 Inspiration Selection&#10;status: completed&#10;selected_count: 3&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="11920" width="232" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step7_step_step8" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step7" target="step_step8"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step8_selected_panel" value="Step 8 Selected Inspiration&#10;count: 3&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: chemical_engineering&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Multiscale process systems engineering—analysis and&#10;design of chemical and energy systems from molecular design up&#10;to process optimization&#10;plausible: True